.TH LIBREG 3 2026-10-19 "Peios peiosutils 0.8.18"
.SH NAME
libreg \- registry batch document library
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <peios/reg.h>

.B "uint32_t peios_reg_abi_version(void);"
.B "peios_reg_document *peios_reg_document_parse(peios_reg_bytes json);"
.B "peios_reg_document *peios_reg_document_export(peios_reg_bytes path);"
.B "peios_reg_apply *peios_reg_document_apply(const peios_reg_document *, peios_reg_bytes layer);"
.B "peios_reg_bytes peios_reg_document_json(const peios_reg_document *);"
.B "void peios_reg_document_free(peios_reg_document *);"
.B "int32_t peios_reg_document_error(const peios_reg_document *);"
.B "peios_reg_bytes peios_reg_document_message(const peios_reg_document *);"
.B "size_t peios_reg_apply_count(const peios_reg_apply *);"
.B "void peios_reg_apply_free(peios_reg_apply *);"
.B "int32_t peios_reg_apply_error(const peios_reg_apply *);"
.B "peios_reg_bytes peios_reg_apply_message(const peios_reg_apply *);"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libreg
parses JSON batch documents, exports live registry subtrees, and applies
batches in one registry transaction.
Parsing owns the document and its normalized JSON representation.
It does not perform the value, path, or access validation required by apply.
.PP
.B peios_reg_document_apply
accepts an absent layer to select the existing default layer.
A non-null empty layer is an explicitly empty layer name.
The separate apply handle reports the number of keys applied on success.
Do not use that count before checking the apply error.
.SH SECURITY
Export and apply use the caller's existing registry authority through libpeios.
They do not create an elevated token or bypass registry access checks.
Errors from the registry are retained in the returned handle.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Operations return an owned handle on success and on failure.
Check the matching error accessor before reading results; zero means success
and a nonzero value is a POSIX error number, independent of errno.
A diagnostic message is borrowed from a failed handle.
A null handle has error EINVAL and no message.
.PP
Free every handle exactly once with its matching free function, including
handles describing failed operations.
Freeing null is harmless.
Returned byte spans and record views remain valid only while their owning
handle remains alive.
Counts return zero for failed or null handles; check the error first.
.PP
Input byte spans are borrowed for the duration of the call.
A null pointer with zero length denotes absence; a non-null zero-length span
is an explicitly empty value.
Lengths exceeding the addressable Rust slice range and null pointers with
nonzero lengths are rejected.
Other non-null pointers must identify valid, readable allocations.
Output pointers and arrays must have the alignment required by their C type.
Text spans contain UTF-8 without a required terminating null byte.
.PP
Read-only access can occur concurrently.
Destruction requires exclusive ownership and ends all borrowed views.
Allocation exhaustion follows the Rust allocator's process-abort policy;
it is not reported as a recoverable ENOMEM result.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
Invalid spans, invalid UTF-8, malformed JSON, invalid document contents,
or an invalid input handle.
Some document validity checks occur only when applying the document.
.TP
.B EIO
A registry operation failed without an available numeric OS error.
.PP
Other registry OS error numbers are returned unchanged.
.SH NOTES
The runtime SONAME is libreg.so.1 and the ABI version function returns 1.
Use the reg pkg-config module to obtain compiler and linker options.
The Rust wrapper is the peios-reg crate.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR reg (1),
.BR libregman (3)
